# Shared Kamal secrets. Committed, because every value here is a `$(...)` command
# reference -- the same text for everyone, resolved on the machine running the
# deploy. No literal secret belongs in this file.
#
# Kamal reads this for EVERY invocation, with or without `-d destination`, and
# layers any per-destination file on top. That is why it is named `secrets-common`
# and not `secrets`: a file called `.kamal/secrets` is only read when NO
# destination is given, so anything in it silently disappears the moment someone
# adds `-d production`. See docs/Deployment.md for the failure that causes.
#
# The Rails API has an identical file. They are separate repositories, so Kamal
# resolves each app's secrets from its own directory -- there is no shared store.

# ECR registry token, same short-lived token the Rails API uses. The username is
# literally "AWS" and there is no long-lived registry password to store; this is
# fetched fresh on every deploy.
#
# This app talks only to the public API surface of Rails and holds nothing of its
# own, so that is the entire list. SECRET_KEY_BASE and JWT_SECRET must never
# appear in the frontend container: anything in it is reachable by the browser.
KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD=$(aws ecr get-login-password --region us-east-1 --profile default)
